{"id":"x67","run":1,"role":"calibration","category":"calendar","question":"Was January 4, 1650 a Tuesday (in the Gregorian calendar)?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":null,"claims":[{"text":"January 4, 1650 in the Gregorian calendar was a Tuesday.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"January 1, 2000 was a Saturday, a reliable anchor.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The 350 years from 1650 to 2000 contain 84 leap days.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The total of 127,834 days is exactly 18,262 weeks, so Jan 1, 1650 fell on the same weekday as Jan 1, 2000.","confidence":0.92,"lean":1,"theta":1.4451,"phi":0.0},{"text":"January 4 is three days after January 1, and Saturday plus three days is Tuesday.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Because the Gregorian calendar was not in use before 1582, the date might be read as Julian, giving a different weekday.","confidence":0.2,"lean":-1,"theta":0.3142,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Historical records from 1650 would normally use the Julian calendar, so a reader might assume the Julian reading.","confidence":0.2,"lean":-1,"theta":0.3142,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The question explicitly specifies the Gregorian calendar, so proleptic Gregorian reckoning governs.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":-0.4,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":-0.3,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":4,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":7,"J":0.2,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":3,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":3,"j":4,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},